<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anastasius I Dicorus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 430–518</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Anastasius I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anastasius I was emperor of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from 491 to 518. A palace official chosen by the widowed empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>, he reformed the coinage and taxation, leaving a large surplus in the treasury, and fought a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from 502 to 506. His sympathy for Miaphysite views provoked revolts, and his treaty of around 502 made the Ghassanids the empire's leading Arab allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus.jpg" alt="Anastasius I Dicorus" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anastasius_I._minted_507-518.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anastasius was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrachium</a> into an Illyro-Roman family, probably no later than 431. He had one black eye and one blue eye, which gave him the nickname Dicorus, meaning two-pupiled. Before becoming emperor he was a silentiary, a palace official. His brother Paulus was consul in 496, and his extensive family included many relatives who held office, among them his nephews Hypatius and Pompeius. After his coronation he proclaimed that his family had a noble Roman lineage descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> the Great, an attempt, according to the historian Anthony Kaldellis, to prove his Romanness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of Zeno in 491, crowds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople">Hippodrome of Constantinople</a> demanded an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Roman emperor. Zeno's widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a> chose Anastasius over Zeno's brother Longinus, which upset the Isaurians. The patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> required him to pledge not to repudiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a>, since Anastasius sympathised with the Monophysites. Ariadne married him on 20 May 491. He abolished the hated tax on receipts and in 498 the chrysargyron tax on merchants and traders. He also had taxes paid in cash rather than goods, paid soldiers in money, and in 498 revived the monetary system with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> and the follis.</p><p>His reign saw two wars. The Isaurian War of 492 to 497 followed Longinus's supporters' revolt, which was broken at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Cotyaeum</a>, and many Isaurians were relocated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. In the Anastasian War of 502 to 505 against Sassanid Persia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Theodosiopolis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> recovered Amida for gold, and peace followed in 506. Anastasius built the fortress of Daras and the Anastasian Wall to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> raids. His moderate Monophysite policy broke down in 512, when he deposed the Metropolitan of Chalcedon and provoked riots. The following year the general Vitalian rebelled and marched on Constantinople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anastasius died childless in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 9 July 518. According to a probably fictional account in the Anonymous Valesianus, he left his succession to chance, and the first person to enter his room the next morning was Justin, commander of the Excubitors, who became emperor. Victor of Tunnuna gives his age at death as 88, a figure most modern historians accept. He left the treasury with 23,000,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a>, or 320,000 pounds of gold, and the stable government and sound finances he created allowed his successors, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, to pursue more ambitious policies. His coinage remained in circulation long after his reign, and four of his solidi have been found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anastasius I Dicorus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperor <b>Anastasius</b> granted him an honorary consulship, and in his last years Clovis eliminated the rulers of the other Frankish groups, such as Sigibert of Cologne, made Paris his chief residence and summoned a church council at Orléans in 511, the year he died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Anastasius I Dicorus</b>, a career civil servant aged 61, became Eastern Roman emperor after being chosen by Ariadne, the widow of his predecessor Zeno.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Entering Roman territory in the late 5th century, they defeated the Salihids and around 502 made a treaty with the emperor <b>Anastasius</b> that made them the empire's chief Arab allies in Syria and Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/ghassanids">Ghassanids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=487" aria-label="Durrës on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 690 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anastasius I Dicorus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=487">What was here in 487 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
